single uniform hashing
======================

n = no of keys in table
m = size of table

load factor alpha = n / m  = expected key per slot (each slot how many values need to be added)
O(1) time


hash functions:
==============

1) division method:
h(k) = k % m
m = prime no (not too close to the power of 2 or 10)

2)multiplication method
h(k) = [(a.k)% 2]>> (w-r)
a = random number
w = no of bits in k
m = 2^r


universal hashing:
=================

h(k) = [(ak+b) % p] % m
a & b are random [0,1,...,p-1]
p is a large prime number


2) open addressing

one item per slot
try next possible item to store

probing strategies:

1)linear probing
 h(k,i) = (h(k) + i ) % m linearly jumping one by one
problem is clustering

2)Double hashing
h(k,i) = (h(k) + i * h2(k)) % m

if h2(k) is relation prime to m for all k.


Interface Map<K,V>:
TreeMap => items stored in order for map
Set = > remove duplicates have the unique elements


HashMap dsa used to store data in key-value pairs. it allows fast insertion , deletion, and searching.

each key is unique. key maps to value

how works:
key passed to hash function
hash function converts key => index (array position)
values stored at index

key features:

fast operations
  insert -> O(1)
  search -> O(1)
  delete -> O(1)

key must be unique . values can be duplicate
Unordered (not maintain the order)

Methods:

1) put => inserts or updates a key-value pair (if key exists value is updated)

map.put("Aara",21);

2)get => retrieve value using key

map.get("Aara") // output : 21

3) remove => deleted key-value pair

map.remove("Aara")

4) containsKey() = > checks if exists
map.containsKey("Aara") // true or false

5) containsValue() => checks if value exists
map.containsValue(21)

6) size() => Returns number of elements
map.size()

7) isEmpty() => checks if map is empty
map.isEmpty()

8) clear() => remove all elements
map.clear()

9) keySet() => returns all keys
map.keySet()

10) values() => Returns all values
map.values()

11) entrySet() => returns key-value pairs

for(Map.entry<String,Integer> entry : map.entrySet()){
  {
    System.out.println(entry.getKey() + " " + entry.getValue())
  }


12) getOrDefault() - returns value or default if key not found
map.getOrDefault("Bob" , 0);

13) putIfAbsent() = > Inserts only if key is not present
map.putIfAbsent("Aara" , 30);

14) replace() => replaces value
map.replace("Aara",50)

15) ForEach() => loop through map

map.foreach((key, value) -> System.out.println(key + " " + value))



Techniques:

1) Frequency counting
 store how many times each elements appears.
 -> use HashMap<Element, count>

 when to use;

         Count duplicates
         find most/ least frequent element
         Anagram problems
         First non-repeating element



 most used function:
    map.put(n, map.getOrDefault(n,0)+1);




2)Fast Look ups:

Use HashMap to check existence instantly O(1) time.
Instead of searching array O(n).

when to use:

        two sum problem
        check duplicates
        Find pair with target
        Membership checking

most used function:

  map.containsKey()


3) Caching / memorization

Store already computed result -> avoid recomputation

improves performance (especially recursion)

when to use:

        Recursion Optimization
        Dynamic Programming
        Fibonacci , Factorial
        Overlapping subproblems


Interview trick:

"count" => frequency Map
"find pair" => Lookup
"optimize recursion" => Memorization



















